    "text": "Given that the arithmetic mean of \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) is 18, we have:\n\\[\n\\frac{x + y}{2} = 18 \\implies x + y = 36.\n\\]\nAlso, given that the geometric mean of \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) is \\(\\sqrt{92}\\), we have:\n\\[\n\\sqrt{xy} = \\sqrt{92} \\implies xy = 92.\n\\]\nWe need to find \\(x^2 + y^2\\). We can use the identity:\n\\[\nx^2 + y^2 = (x + y)^2 - 2xy.\n\\]\nSubstituting the values of \\(x + y\\) and \\(xy\\) into the identity, we get:\n\\[\nx^2 + y^2 = 36^2 - 2 \\cdot 92 = 1296 - 184 = 1112.\n\\]\nThus, the value of \\(x^2 + y^2\\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{1112}.\n\\]",
